good morning--
this inquiry raised a question i've been meaning to ask involving 
matching in a multilevel context--

Suppose one has children in a classroom, some of which are exposed to 
some "treatment" and others are not. 

i can see two options but would like a third
1) add the classroom identifier as a fixed- or random-effect to the 
model generating the propensity
(if one used the former, there would be some issues with fixed-effect 
logit, such as having to drop classrooms where all or no kids got the tx)

the propensity score would capture the effect of the classroom 
identifier in the same way it does other covariates

2) forcing the match to exactly match on that covariate (i.e., matching 
only within classrooms)

are there other options?  (2) seems too stringent, yet (1) just doesn't 
seem to capture the qualitative differences among classrooms.  For 
example, we might boost the propensity score for a given case ten 
percentage points because the treatment is more common in that 
classroom.  the effect would be equivalent to changing the value of some 
other covariate.  i guess that's really what the propensity score really 
assumes.

maybe part of what is bothering me about this scenario is SUTVA and 
maybe that is contributing to my unease.  any one have any insights?

regards,
michael


